  "source_brief": "# LS-1 Landing Pad, Short Sortie\n\n**Site.** LS-1 plateau near the lunar south pole, same footprint as the baseline landing-pad job. Loose regolith extends to 40 cm and must be removed across the whole pad footprint; the berm needs a key trench cut into firm material because the plateau slopes north. The depot is on the south edge of the site.\n\n**Structures.**\n\n1. Landing pad: 20 m diameter bowl excavated 0.6 m to firm regolith across the full footprint, with a compacted subgrade. Sintering is deferred to a later sortie when the printer arrives.\n2. Blast berm: 60 m arc, 2.5 m high, 4 m at the base, on a 0.5 m deep key trench, built from pad spoil and compacted.\n3. Approach apron: strip a 30 m by 8 m lane on the south side so the hauler can reach the pad.\n\n**Fleet.** EX1 and EX2 (excavators, 5000 Wh each), HL1 (hauler, 3000 Wh), CP1 (compactor, 3500 Wh) and SV1 (surveyor, 1500 Wh). No printer. No recharging; each battery is the budget for the whole sortie.\n\n**Deadline.** 7 days.\n\n**Operational constraints.**\n\n- Excavation dominates this sortie: close to 100 cubic metres of regolith must be moved, so both excavators share the dig and no robot may be loaded beyond its battery.\n- Only one robot may work inside a zone at a time.\n- Berm compaction waits for the pad bowl, which supplies the fill.\n"
 },
